    t/mean.t: very large but close integers

    I don't like how there's nothing I can do when one of
    the operands gets promoted to NV because the comparison
    would then be done with floating points, which itself
    is faulty because ULP is too permissive!

    I'd probably like having a test that explicitly checks
    that integers stay integers better, but then again, that
    would break encapsulation because that is just not true
    in perl v5.6 (which (if my guess is correct) converts NV
    back to IV/UV when it's used as one, e.g. as array index).

    As for testing at the boundary of float precision (53 on
    x86_64), this should cover it since IV is much larger than
    the maximum NV with ULP<=1.0; while still fitting within
    UV over two times.  This should confidentently weed out
    faulty implementations like int(($lo + $hi) / 2). (Not
    so much the more defensive $lo + int(($hi - $lo) / 2),
    but that is covered by [ivmin,ivmax) and [ivmin,uvmax).)

